.TH "DOCUMENT.CREATEELEMENT" "3JS" "April 6, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Document.createElement \- Document: createElement() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]createElement()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]Document\f[R] interface creates a new \f[CR]HTMLElement\f[R] that
has the specified \f[CR]localName\f[R].
.PP
If \f[CR]localName\f[R] isn\(cqt recognized, the method creates an
\f[CR]HTMLUnknownElement\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
createElement(localName)
createElement(localName, options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]localName\f[R]
A string that specifies the type of element to be created.
Don\(cqt use qualified names (like \(lqhtml:a\(rq) with this method.
When called on an HTML document, \f[CR]createElement()\f[R] converts
\f[CR]localName\f[R] to lowercase before creating the element.
In Firefox, Opera, and Chrome, \f[CR]createElement(null)\f[R] works like
\f[CR]createElement(\(dqnull\(dq)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An object with the following optional properties (note that only one of
\f[CR]is\f[R] and \f[CR]customElementRegistry\f[R] may be set):
.RS
.TP
\f[B]is\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A string defining the tag name for a custom element previously defined
using \f[CR]customElements.define()\f[R].
The new element will be given an \f[CR]is\f[R] attribute whose value is
the custom element\(cqs tag name.
See Web component example for more details.
.TP
\f[B]customElementRegistry\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A \f[CR]CustomElementRegistry\f[R] that sets the Scoped custom element
registry of a custom element.
.RE
.SS Return value
The new \f[CR]Element\f[R].
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] A new HTMLElement is returned if the document is an
HTMLDocument, which is the most common case.
Otherwise a new Element is returned.
.RE
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]InvalidCharacterError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if the \f[CR]localName\f[R] value is not a valid element name.
A string is a valid element name if its length is at least 1 and:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
it starts with an alphabet character and does not contain ASCII
whitespace, \f[CR]NULL\f[R], \f[CR]/\f[R], or \f[CR]>\f[R] (U+0000,
U+002F, or U+003E, respectively).
.IP \(bu 2
it starts with \f[CR]:\f[R] (U+003A), \f[CR]_\f[R] (U+005F), or any
characters in the range U+0080 to U+10FFFF (inclusive), \f[I]and\f[R]
the remaining code points only include those same characters along with
the ASCII alphanumeric characters, \f[CR]\-\f[R] (U+002D), and
\f[CR].\f[R] (U+002E),
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Earlier versions of the specification were more
restrictive, requiring that the \f[CR]localName\f[R] be a valid \c
.UR https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/#dt-name
XML name
.UE \c
\&.
.RE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]NotSupportedError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if both the \f[CR]is\f[R] and \f[CR]customElementRegistry\f[R]
options are specified.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic example
This creates a new \f[CR]<div>\f[R] and inserts it before the element
with the ID \f[CR]div1\f[R].
.SS HTML
.IP
.EX
<!doctype html>
<\f[B]html\f[R] lang=\(dqen\-US\(dq>
  <\f[B]head\f[R]>
    <\f[B]meta\f[R] charset=\(dqUTF\-8\(dq />
    <\f[B]title\f[R]>Working with elements</\f[B]title\f[R]>
  </\f[B]head\f[R]>
  <\f[B]body\f[R]>
    <\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqdiv1\(dq>The text above has been created dynamically.</\f[B]div\f[R]>
  </\f[B]body\f[R]>
</\f[B]html\f[R]>
.EE
.SS JavaScript
.IP
.EX
\f[B]function\f[R] addElement() {
  \f[I]// create a new div element\f[R]
  \f[B]const\f[R] newDiv = document.createElement(\(dqdiv\(dq);

  \f[I]// and give it some content\f[R]
  \f[B]const\f[R] newContent = document.createTextNode(\(dqHi there and greetings!\(dq);

  \f[I]// add the text node to the newly created div\f[R]
  newDiv.appendChild(newContent);

  \f[I]// add the newly created element and its content into the DOM\f[R]
  \f[B]const\f[R] currentDiv = document.getElementById(\(dqdiv1\(dq);
  document.body.insertBefore(newDiv, currentDiv);
}

addElement();
.EE
.SS Result
.SS Web component example
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Check the browser compatibility section for support, and
the \f[CR]is\f[R] attribute reference for caveats on implementation
reality of customized built\-in elements.
.RE
.PP
The following example snippet is taken from our \c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/web-components-examples/tree/main/expanding-list-web-component
expanding\-list\-web\-component
.UE \c
\ example (\c
.UR https://mdn.github.io/web-components-examples/expanding-list-web-component/
see it live also
.UE \c
).
In this case, our custom element extends the
\f[CR]HTMLUListElement\f[R], which represents the \f[CR]<ul>\f[R]
element.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Create a class for the element\f[R]
\f[B]class\f[R] ExpandingList \f[B]extends\f[R] HTMLUListElement {
  constructor() {
    \f[I]// Always call super first in constructor\f[R]
    \f[B]super\f[R]();

    \f[I]// constructor definition left out for brevity\f[R]
    \f[I]// \&...\f[R]
  }
}

\f[I]// Define the new element\f[R]
customElements.define(\(dqexpanding\-list\(dq, ExpandingList, { extends: \(dqul\(dq });
.EE
.PP
If we wanted to create an instance of this element programmatically,
we\(cqd use a call along the following lines:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]let\f[R] expandingList = document.createElement(\(dqul\(dq, { is: \(dqexpanding\-list\(dq });
.EE
.PP
The new element will be given an \f[CR]is\f[R] attribute whose value is
the custom element\(cqs tag name.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] For backwards compatibility, some browsers will allow
you to pass a string here instead of an object, where the string\(cqs
value is the custom element\(cqs tag name.
.RE
